7.1. "Monetization" means making the Software, or access to its functionality, an identifiable part of a paid product, paid entitlement, commercial offering, or revenue-generating service, regardless of whether net profit is achieved. It includes: (a) charging for copies or access; (b) charging license, subscription, or membership fees for the Software or its functionality; (c) offering the Software as a paid hosted or managed service; (d) including advertising, paid promotions, or sponsorships inside the Software for revenue; (e) charging consideration specifically for preinstallation, inclusion, promotion, or integration of the Software; or (f) offering access to the Software as a material, separately charged benefit of another commercial offering.

7.2. The following do not constitute Monetization merely by occurring: (a) ordinary personal or organizational use, including use by a business or its employees; (b) free distribution of unmodified Software; (c) general advertising on a separate website offering free unmodified downloads, without altering or paywalling the Software; (d) free preinstallation on a device that is itself sold; (e) genuinely voluntary donations or grants that confer no paid features, access conditions, or preferential software entitlements; (f) reasonable, separately identified reimbursement of actual media, delivery, installation-assistance, or similar ancillary costs, without charging for the Software itself; (g) sale or licensing of independently created extensions; and (h) operation of an independent music, storage, or media service merely because a customer chooses to access it using the Software.

7.3. Section 7.2 does not cover arrangements whose actual substance is payment for the Software, its hosted execution, or its functionality—regardless of whether the charges are labeled as support, maintenance, consulting, or service fees.

7.4. Monetization that relies on copyright permissions beyond this License requires separate written commercial authorization from the relevant rights holder. Where a separate enforceable contract applies, Monetization obligations are governed by that contract and applicable law. Nothing here prohibits activities that do not legally require the rights holder's permission.

